+| Status chip | `Quickshell.Services.Pipewire`, `Quickshell.Bluetooth`, `Quickshell.Networking` | opens quick settings. While a device that reports its battery is connected, its level (terracotta at 20 % or less) replaces the Bluetooth icon (owner's request); a crossed-out mic shows while the default input is muted, and a mic in `clay` while an app records from a microphone (owner's request, after phase 8: a privacy cue, orange as on phones). `Audio.recorders` are the capture streams that don't record an output (`stream.capture.sink`); Quickshell reads capture streams as not sinks, the opposite of playback streams (checked with `pw-record`). A screen shared through the portal adds a screen icon in `clay` (owner's request, after phase 8): `ScreenShare` finds xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland's video source nodes (named `xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland`, checked with a real Vesktop share) and the streams linked to them (`Pipewire.linkGroups`), whose apps it names; a webcam's source isn't the portal's, and the shell's own captures don't use PipeWire |
+| Quick settings | the same services, plus `Brightness` (`ddcutil`), `NightLight` (`hyprsunset`), `Notifs` (do not disturb) and `Uptime` (`/proc/uptime`) | a layer-shell `PanelWindow`, not a `PopupWindow`: grabbing popups fail unless opened by a click on the bar, so keybinds could not open it. `FocusGrab` (adapter wrapper of `HyprlandFocusGrab`) closes it on an outside click. Keybind: `qs -c pistachio ipc call quicksettings toggle`. A media card (owner's request; not on a board) sits between the tiles and the sliders while an MPRIS player has something: rounded cover art (`ClippingRectangle`), title, artist, previous / play-pause / next, dimmed when the player doesn't offer them; `Media` picks the player that is playing, else the one that played last. No progress bar: MPRIS position doesn't update on its own. Right-clicking the Night light tile opens its schedule over the panel (owner's request; not on a board): a switch and two `TimeStepper`s, 15-minute steps; `NightLight` switches only when the schedule's answer changes, so a manual toggle holds until the next change, and the tile reads "Until 07:00" / "From 20:00". Right-clicking the Do not disturb tile opens its schedule the same way (owner's request, after phase 8; not on a board): a switch, "Night light's times" (a switch: follow night light's from and to, whether or not night light's own schedule is on) and, while that's off, its own two times; `Notifs` follows it with the same rule and status texts, and the bar's clock tooltip says "until 07:00". The Bluetooth tile shows the connected device's battery. Clicking the volume icon mutes and unmutes. A Microphone slider sits under Volume while there's a default input (owner's request, after phase 8); clicking its icon mutes the mic. The scroll wheel over a slider moves it 5 % a notch, warmth 250 K, as the launcher's ← → (owner's request, after phase 8): `components/Slider.qml` sets the control's own `wheelEnabled`, which follows a touchpad smoothly and emits `moved` like a drag. A search button left of the power button opens the launcher (owner's request). Right-clicking the Bluetooth tile opens quick connections over the panel (owner's request; not on a board): the power switch, up to five paired devices (click to connect or disconnect) and "Pair new devices…", which opens the launcher's Bluetooth view (the request goes through `Bar` to `shell.qml`, which owns the launcher). Both right-click cards use `components/Popover.qml` (the dimmed panel and the card), and `popup` makes sure only one shows. Checked by `tests/services.sh` |
 | Remembered settings | `Store` (`FileView` + `JsonAdapter` in `Quickshell.statePath("state.json")`) | Do not disturb, night light on/off, warmth and schedule, and notification history, across restarts. `ready` turns true once the file is read (or found missing); nothing is saved before it, and saves wait 300 ms for a slider drag to settle. Named `Store`: a singleton called `State` is shadowed by QtQuick's `State` type. The test shell has its own state folder |
-| Notifications | `Quickshell.Services.Notifications` | we become the notification daemon, so any other daemon must not run. `Notifs` holds the history, popups, seen/unread and do-not-disturb (which hides popups but keeps notifications). Body text is shown as plain text. Do not disturb lets critical notifications through. History survives restarts: notifications from before come back from `Store` as plain records (no actions, only file images), and ones a live reload already kept are matched by summary, body and app and not shown twice. Toasts sit centred over the bar, 16 px above it (owner's call: right-aligned looked off centre), newest nearest the bar, 5 s each (or the app's timeout, which arrives in milliseconds although the 0.3.2 docs say seconds); critical ones stay. Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call notifications toggle` / `toggleDnd`. Checked by `tests/notifications.sh` Pointing at a card in the centre shows the app's actions under its text (owner's request, after phase 8; not on the board): the card grows as they fade in, and scrolls into view at the bottom of the list. The first is pistachio, as on the toasts (`components/ActionButton.qml`, shared). An action on a notification that isn't resident swipes the card away first, then runs, since the notification is destroyed after it; records from before a restart have no actions. The centre groups notifications by app (owner's request, after phase 8; not on the board): the app with the newest first, newest first within it. A group of two or more gets a header on its newest card, laid out like the launcher's section headers (`DISCORD · 4`, Show all / Show less, ✕ for that app's notifications, through `Notifs.clearApp`), and starts folded: the other cards fold to nothing (each card keeps its own 10 px gap, so the list's spacing is 0) and the edges of the next two peek 6 px out under the newest; clicking them opens the group too, and so does a click anywhere on the header (owner's request). A group stays open or folded as it was left until the shell restarts (owner's request; it used to fold again on each opening), and a group that empties forgets, so the app's next notifications start folded. Folding, opening and the header all ease. The list's model is the notifications in that order, so the cards keep their add, swipe and displaced motion; the delegate is an item around the card, which carries the swipe. |
+| Notifications | `Quickshell.Services.Notifications` | we become the notification daemon, so any other daemon must not run. `Notifs` holds the history, popups, seen/unread and do-not-disturb (which hides popups but keeps notifications). Body text is shown as plain text. Do not disturb lets critical notifications through. History survives restarts: notifications from before come back from `Store` as plain records (no actions, only file images), and ones a live reload already kept are matched by summary, body and app and not shown twice. Toasts sit centred over the bar, 16 px above it (owner's call: right-aligned looked off centre), newest nearest the bar, 5 s each (or the app's timeout, which arrives in milliseconds although the 0.3.2 docs say seconds); critical ones stay. Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call notifications toggle` / `toggleDnd`. Checked by `tests/notifications.sh` Pointing at a card in the centre shows the app's actions under its text (owner's request, after phase 8; not on the board): the card grows as they fade in, and scrolls into view at the bottom of the list. The first is pistachio, as on the toasts (`components/ActionButton.qml`, shared). An action on a notification that isn't resident swipes the card away first, then runs, since the notification is destroyed after it; records from before a restart have no actions. The centre groups notifications by app (owner's request, after phase 8; not on the board): the app with the newest first, newest first within it. A group of two or more gets a header on its newest card, laid out like the launcher's section headers (`DISCORD · 4`, Show all / Show less, ✕ for that app's notifications, through `Notifs.clearApp`), and starts folded: the other cards fold to nothing (each card keeps its own 10 px gap, so the list's spacing is 0) and the edges of the next two peek 6 px out under the newest; clicking them opens the group too, and so does a click anywhere on the header (owner's request). A group stays open or folded as it was left until the shell restarts (owner's request; it used to fold again on each opening), and a group that empties forgets, so the app's next notifications start folded. Folding, opening and the header all ease. The list's model is the notifications in that order, so the cards keep their add, swipe and displaced motion; the delegate is an item around the card, which carries the swipe. Toasts show the time they have left as a 3 px `overlay2` bar along the bottom, which stops while the pointer is over the toast and then never has less than 1.5 s (owner's request, after phase 8). Per-app rules (owner's request, after phase 8), by app name in `Store`: a right click on a toast or a card turns its button row into Silence 1 h, No toasts (or their undo) and Cancel; the app's notifications still reach the centre, critical ones still toast, and launcher → System → Notification rules lists them to undo. `Bluetooth` warns, through the shell's own notify-send, when a connected device's battery reaches 20 %, and again, critical, at 10 %; once per discharge. History (owner's request, after phase 8): the last 200 are saved (50 before the launcher could search them), and any older than a week go by themselves, from the centre too (`Notifs.pruneOld`, each minute); one whose time isn't known stays. The launcher's `!` finds one, and `NotificationCentre.show(n)` (through `Bar.showNotification`) opens the centre at it: its group unfolds if folded, and after the cards have moved the list scrolls it into view and the card turns `overlay2` for 1.5 s |
 | Calendar | `SystemClock` (hourly, for the date change at midnight) | Built after phase 8 (owner's request; board `calendar-notifications`). `panels/Calendar.qml`, a 300 px panel left of the notification centre, which shrinks from 400 to 360 px; the two sit 16 px apart in one window centred over the bar (the pair is wider than the bar) and are both as tall as the calendar, so the centre's list scrolls in the space left, about three notifications (owner's call after building: the board let the list grow to 60 % of the screen, which stretched the calendar into empty space). Dates only (owner's call: no events or holidays). Weeks start on Monday whatever the locale (en_US would start on Sunday), six rows always so the panel keeps its height; days of the months around it in `subtext`, today as a pistachio chip with `bg` text. ‹ ›, the scroll wheel (a notch, or a touchpad's steps adding up to one), ← → or h l change month; Today or Home go back, and every opening starts at the current month. The months slide (owner's request; not on the board): the new title and grid come in 36 px (12 for the title) as the old ones carry on and fade, sideways for ‹ › ← → and Today, up and down for the wheel, 200 ms ease-out. Each has two copies that take turns (`panels/MonthPage.qml`; as an inline component it was "not a type"), so a fast wheel never waits; the grid clips them, and opening jumps without a slide. Days aren't clickable. The grid comes from `panels/calendar.js` (tested by `tests/calendar.test.js`). With no notifications the list's space shows an empty-inbox icon in `sageBlue` on an `overlay` chip, "Inbox zero" and "Nothing new here. Enjoy the quiet." (owner's request; not on the board). The centre's list fades out over its last 18 % while there's more below, with a round ↓ button on the fade that slides to the oldest notification (owner's request; not on the board). Motion in the list (owner's request; not on the board): a wheel notch glides 100 px (200 ms ease-out) instead of jumping, while a touchpad scrolls the list directly; ✕ swipes the card out to the right as it fades, then the cards below slide up into the gap (`displaced`); Clear all swipes the cards on screen out from the top down, starting up to 120 ms apart, then empties the list, and the empty state fades in rising 8 px; a new card fades and grows in. A card plays its exit before dismissing (as toasts do), because a dismissed live notification is destroyed while its card is still there, nulling `modelData`; the card then falls back to a blank record. Whether there's more below is worked out from `contentY`, `height` and `contentHeight`: with `atYEnd` the button once stayed hidden after a reload |
-| OSD | `Audio` (PipeWire) and `Brightness` | `popups/Osd.qml`, 320 × 56, click-through, in the overlay layer. Volume shows on any PipeWire change, whoever made it, except in the first second and for a second after the output device changes; a new default output shows its name, a headphones or speaker icon and its volume, for 2.5 s (built after phase 8; not on the board); the microphone, for 2.5 s, when an app starts recording (naming the apps recording, clay mic icon, or the slashed one with "muted") and when the last one stops (owner's request; not on the board): a change counts once it has lasted a second, since apps open the mic briefly to look at devices, and it shows even with quick settings open, in Do not disturb or fullscreen (a privacy cue); brightness only through IPC, because the monitor reports nothing. It sits where the pill is while the bar is hidden, else 16 px above the bar, and stays away while quick settings (same sliders) is open. Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call brightness up` / `down` (5 %); volume keys keep calling `wpctl` |
+| OSD | `Audio` (PipeWire) and `Brightness` | `popups/Osd.qml`, 320 × 56, click-through, in the overlay layer. Volume shows on any PipeWire change, whoever made it, except in the first second and for a second after the output device changes; a new default output shows its name, a headphones or speaker icon and its volume, for 2.5 s (built after phase 8; not on the board); the microphone or the shared screen, for 2.5 s, when an app starts recording or sharing (naming the apps recording, clay mic icon, or the slashed one with "muted") and when the last one stops (owner's request; not on the board): a change counts once it has lasted a second, since apps open the mic briefly to look at devices, and it shows even with quick settings open, in Do not disturb or fullscreen (a privacy cue); brightness only through IPC, because the monitor reports nothing. It sits where the pill is while the bar is hidden, else 16 px above the bar, and stays away while quick settings (same sliders) is open. Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call brightness up` / `down` (5 %); volume keys keep calling `wpctl` |
 | Power menu | `Session` (`systemctl suspend` / `reboot` / `poweroff` via `Quickshell.execDetached`; Suspend locks first and waits for the lock to be confirmed, at most 2 s; `extras/hypridle.conf` covers idle locking and other suspends; log out through `Compositor.exit()`, Hyprland's exit dispatcher) | `panels/PowerMenu.qml`, full screen in the overlay layer, exclusive keyboard. The menu sits centred over a blurred, dimmed still of the screen (owner's request; not on the board): a `ScreencopyView` taken as it opens, blurred with `MultiEffect` (`QtQuick.Effects`) and dimmed with `bg` at 45 %. It waits for the still (at most 300 ms) so the still never contains the menu. Letter keys (L, E, S, R, P) act at once; ← → and Enter pick one; Esc or a click on the background closes. Opened by the power button in quick settings, or `qs -c pistachio ipc call power toggle` |
-| Lock screen | `WlSessionLock` (`Quickshell.Wayland`), `PamContext` (`Quickshell.Services.Pam`) in `Session` | `lock/Lock.qml`. Hyprland supports the session-lock protocol (checked in phase 7). The password goes through PAM's `login` stack, as other lockers do, so pam_faillock applies: three wrong passwords in 15 min lock the account for 10 min, the TTY included. If the shell dies while locked, the screen stays locked; from a TTY run `hyprctl --instance 0 eval 'hl.clear_crashed_lockscreen()'` (tested). Keybind: `qs -c pistachio ipc call lock lock`. Polish after phase 8 (owner's request; not on the board): once locked, the compositor shows only the lock, so a still of the desktop is taken just before: a click-through overlay shows a `ScreencopyView` of the screen (which looks the same) and `grabToImage` keeps it in memory; the lock waits for it, at most 300 ms, else goes up over the plain background (so does a live reload while locked). The lock's first frame is that still, sharp; over `growDuration` the blur and a 0.6 `bg` dim fade in and the clock and field settle in from 1.04× scale. A soft dark patch (`RectangularShadow`, `bg` at 45 %) behind the clock and field keeps the date and hint readable over a bright desktop (about 3.5:1 for `subtext` even over white). After the right password the lock stays up for `growDuration` while all of it fades back to the sharp still, growing to 1.04×. The field draws the password as 8 px dots that pop in (a `ListModel`, so only the new dot animates), with its own blinking pistachio caret; the dots pulse while PAM checks, and a wrong password shakes the field and fades them out. An empty Enter is ignored (it would cost a pam_faillock strike). What's playing shows above the status capsule (`components/MediaCard.qml`, shared with quick settings). The power menu runs its action once it has faded out, so the still never shows it |
+| Lock screen | `WlSessionLock` (`Quickshell.Wayland`), `PamContext` (`Quickshell.Services.Pam`) in `Session` | `lock/Lock.qml`. Hyprland supports the session-lock protocol (checked in phase 7). The password goes through PAM's `login` stack, as other lockers do, so pam_faillock applies: three wrong passwords in 15 min lock the account for 10 min, the TTY included. If the shell dies while locked, the screen stays locked; from a TTY run `hyprctl --instance 0 eval 'hl.clear_crashed_lockscreen()'` (tested). Keybind: `qs -c pistachio ipc call lock lock`. Polish after phase 8 (owner's request; not on the board): once locked, the compositor shows only the lock, so a still of the desktop is taken just before: a click-through overlay shows a `ScreencopyView` of the screen (which looks the same) and `grabToImage` keeps it in memory; the lock waits for it, at most 300 ms, else goes up over the plain background (so does a live reload while locked). The status capsule (the board's row, 14 px gaps) adds a bell and "3 new notifications" while some came that the centre hasn't shown (`Notifs.unreadCount`, as the clock chip's dot), and widens for it (owner's request, after phase 8: the count only, for privacy). The lock's first frame is that still, sharp; over `growDuration` the blur and a 0.6 `bg` dim fade in and the clock and field settle in from 1.04× scale. A soft dark patch (`RectangularShadow`, `bg` at 45 %) behind the clock and field keeps the date and hint readable over a bright desktop (about 3.5:1 for `subtext` even over white). After the right password the lock stays up for `growDuration` while all of it fades back to the sharp still, growing to 1.04×. The field draws the password as 8 px dots that pop in (a `ListModel`, so only the new dot animates), with its own blinking pistachio caret; the dots pulse while PAM checks, and a wrong password shakes the field and fades them out. An empty Enter is ignored (it would cost a pam_faillock strike). What's playing shows above the status capsule (`components/MediaCard.qml`, shared with quick settings). The power menu runs its action once it has faded out, so the still never shows it |
+| Weather | `Weather` (Open-Meteo over `XMLHttpRequest`: its geocoding finds the city in `Store`, `weatherCity`, once; the forecast every 30 min, °C and km/h, WMO codes to words and icons) | Built after phase 8 (owner's request; boards `calendar-weather-notifications`, `weather-details-expanded`, the owner's design). `panels/WeatherPanel.qml` sits above the calendar and the notification centre in their window, as wide as both, once there's a reading: an icon disc (the sun `gold`, rain `sageBlue`, clouds and the night `subtext`, storms `lavender`), the temperature at 40 px, the words, "City · H · L", and five days after a line; the chevron opens the next hours ("Now" on `overlay`, the chance of rain in `sageBlue` from 10 %) and four chips (feels like, wind, humidity, sunset). The window keeps room for the details open, the panels sit at its bottom, so opening them grows the panel without resizing the window. IPC `weather city <name>`, `weather refresh`. The launcher's Shell settings card has a Weather location row (owner's request, after phase 8), showing `Weather.placeName` ("Kłobuck, Poland"): Enter makes way for a question, as a power action does, the city is typed into the search field, and Save runs `Weather.lookup` first; an unknown name or no answer keeps the question open with the problem in terracotta, a found one is saved as Open-Meteo names it (in `Store`). The launcher's `editing` is general (a text setting's title, hint, check and apply), for others later. An answer for a city or place that has changed since it was asked is dropped: the first lookup goes out before `Store` has read the saved city, and once came back after it, showing Warsaw |
+| Wallpaper | `Wallpapers` (a `FolderListModel` of `~/Pictures/Wallpapers`, PNG, JPG and WebP, by name; the one in use and each image's fit in `Store`) | Built after phase 8 (owner's request; boards `wallpaper-switcher`, `wallpaper-switcher-empty`, the owner's design). The shell draws the wallpaper: `panels/Wallpaper.qml`, a full-screen window on the background layer, so hyprpaper retires. `components/WallpaperView.qml` cross-fades: each new image fades in over `growDuration` on top of what shows, and the ones under it go once covered, so quick browsing stays smooth; Fill, Fit, Center and Tile are `PreserveAspectCrop`, `PreserveAspectFit`, `Pad` and `Tile`, `bg` where a fit leaves room. `panels/WallpaperSwitcher.qml` opens from the launcher's Wallpapers row (Shell settings) or IPC `wallpaper toggle` (`random`, `set <path>`): over the windows, it shows the chosen image full-screen as a live preview behind a panel of cards 232 px wide, three across (more than three rows scroll), with "In use" on the current one, the full file name in the pistachio chip when chosen, and a footer with the name, size in pixels and bytes, and the fit switch. Arrows or h j k l preview, Enter or a click sets, R previews a random one, Esc cancels; the pointer chooses only once it moves (as Alt+Tab learnt). No images: a dialog with the folder and Open folder |
+| Window switcher | `Compositor` (the windows, with each one's Wayland toplevel), `Apps` (icons and names) | Built after phase 8 from the owner's own boards (`window-switcher`, `window-switcher-many`). `panels/WindowSwitcher.qml`, IPC `switcher next`, `prev`, `commit`, `cancel`, for Alt+Tab and Alt+Shift+Tab binds. An overlay with the screen dimmed (`bg` at 66 %) and a panel centred in the room above the bar: cards 184 px wide, five to a row, most recently used first (Hyprland's focus history, taken as it opens), opening on the second. Each card has a still of its window (a `ScreencopyView` of its toplevel, covering a 168 × 104 box; other workspaces too), "WS n" for another workspace, a terracotta dot for an urgent one, and its icon and title, in the pistachio chip when chosen. The footer has the full title, "App · Workspace n" and "2 of 5"; a hint line goes under the panel. Releasing Alt switches (`Keys.onReleased`; owner to confirm it's seen, else a release bind on Alt calls `switcher commit`); Tab, Shift+Tab, ← → h l and ↑ ↓ j k move, Enter or a click switches, Q closes the chosen window, Esc or a click outside cancels. Cards look their window up live, so a title change doesn't retake the still. The order is `Compositor`'s own focus order (from `activewindowv2` events; Hyprland's `focusHistoryID` lagged a switch made from here). Committing focuses the window before the switcher lets go of the keyboard, else Hyprland handed it back to the old window; and the pointer chooses a card only once it moves, since the switcher appears under it (both were why letting go of Alt first seemed not to switch) |
+| Screenshots | `Screenshots`, `Compositor` (the windows, for Window) | Built after phase 8 (owner's request; not on a board). `popups/ScreenshotPicker.qml`, IPC `screenshot region` and `screenshot menu`, for Hyprland keybinds. Either takes a still of the screen first (`ScreencopyView`, as the lock screen does) and shows it full-screen, so what's saved is the screen as it was, without the picker: Region drags out an area (crosshair; the rest dims to `bg` at 50 %, a 1 px `textBright` edge and a size chip); Window highlights the window under the pointer (the most recently focused one where they overlap) and a click takes its box; Screen takes it all. The menu is laid out like the power menu (R, W, S, ← → and Enter, Esc). The part is cut from the still by a `ShaderEffectSource` over it (whole pixels, so it looks no different) and `grabToImage().saveToFile()` writes it to `~/Pictures/Screenshots/Screenshot_<date>_<time>.png` (or the localised Pictures folder from `xdg-user-dir`); no `grim` needed. `Screenshots` then sends its own notification through `notify-send`, which waits for the action: a click copies the PNG (`wl-copy`), Open runs `xdg-open`, Delete `gio trash`. Its `x-pistachio-screenshot` hint makes `Notifs.own()` true, so it shows even while quiet, gets the Region glyph and a 16:9 preview (`components/NotificationPreview.qml`, toasts and centre; saved records keep the path). notify-send runs under `setpriv --pdeathsig`, like `pactl`; a reload ends it, so older screenshot notifications lose their buttons |
 | Polkit dialog | `Polkit`, which runs `services/polkit-agent.py` (python-gobject with polkit's `PolkitAgent` bindings) | Built after phase 8 (owner's request; boards `polkit-prompt`, `polkit-details`, `polkit-wrong-password`). Not Quickshell's `PolkitAgent`: it starts PAM the moment a request arrives, so even Cancel counted as a failed login for pam_faillock (checked: one strike per cancel). The shell's agent registers with polkit for the login session and starts PAM (polkit's setuid helper, through `PolkitAgent.Session`) only once a password is submitted, so Esc and Cancel cost nothing; Cancel waits while PAM checks a password, since stopping it then would count. The agent also names the app that asked (`polkit.subject-pid`, else `polkit.caller-pid`, from `/proc`) and the action's vendor (polkit's action list), which Quickshell's agent doesn't pass. `panels/PolkitDialog.qml`: full screen in the overlay layer, exclusive keyboard, the 440 px card over `bg` at 66 %; a click beside the card does nothing, so a stray one can't dismiss a request. Enter authenticates ("Checking…" while PAM decides), Esc cancels; a wrong password clears the field and shows PAM's own message if it sent one (e.g. the account is locked), else "Wrong password. Try again."; a second PAM question (e.g. a one-time code) replaces the placeholder. Requests queue and show one at a time. A live reload restarts the agent (singletons don't survive it). Checked by `tests/polkit-agent.sh` (no PAM) and by hand with `pkexec /usr/bin/true` |
-| Launcher | `Apps` (wraps `DesktopEntries`), `Compositor`, the services | `launcher/Launcher.qml` builds each mode as a flat list of rows: All (quick controls, open windows; with a query: apps, windows, system), Apps, Windows, Workspaces (cards with a mini map of the real layout), System (cards for audio, night light and Bluetooth, app volumes, output devices), and Bluetooth inside System (power switch, paired devices with connect/disconnect, nearby named devices with pair; Ctrl+Backspace forgets, not plain Backspace, which edits the search). It scans only while the Bluetooth view is open, for at most a minute. Pairing questions from the agent (`Bluetooth.request`, built after phase 8; not on a board) take the place of the results like a power question and open the launcher in the Bluetooth view: a code to compare, a code to type on the device (with a pistachio cursor under the next key), or "Pair with …?" / "Let … use audio?", with Cancel or Decline chosen first; a PIN or passkey is typed into the search field, which takes only valid characters then and is read-only during the other questions; Tab is ignored until it's answered, and Esc or closing the launcher declines. Fuzzy match with highlighted letters in `launcher/fuzzy.js` (tested by `tests/fuzzy.test.js`). Windows and workspaces are snapshotted when it opens, so rows only change when you type or switch mode; the list is a plain array, because a `ScriptModel` left the previous mode's rows on screen. Terminal apps are wrapped in `$TERMINAL` (else kitty). System mode is built of cards (owner's request; the boards show plain rows): Audio (volume, brightness), Night light (a switch row with its status, warmth, a Schedule switch row and the two times) and Bluetooth (a switch row with the connected device and battery, and "Scan for devices", which opens the Bluetooth view). A card's rows stay separate list rows, each drawing its slice of the card's outline, so ↑↓, ←→ (warmth ±250 K, times ±15 min; the wheel over a slider does the same) and Enter (switches) work as everywhere else; a query shows a whole card when it matches its name or any row. It also lists each app playing sound under Apps, with its own volume slider (←→, Enter mutes); the Audio card has a Microphone slider (Enter mutes); Input device lists the microphones, like Output device (Enter makes one the default, through `Pipewire.preferredDefaultAudioSource`); a System monitor card shows CPU (load, temperature, model), memory and an NVIDIA GPU (load, temperature, VRAM) as meters in `sageBlue` that ease to each reading. Its rows are readings, so the keys skip them, and `SystemStats` runs `services/stats.sh` every 1.5 s only while the card is on screen (owner's request, after phase 8). It ends with Shell settings, a card with the Drop shadows switch (owner's request; not on a board) and Quiet in fullscreen (owner's request, after phase 8: while the focused window is fullscreen, `Compositor.fullscreen` from its `Toplevel`, `Notifs.quiet` holds toasts back like Do not disturb, critical ones excepted; on by default, kept in `Store`). Apps mode opens with Frequent, the five apps launched most and most lately (owner's request, after phase 8): `Apps.launch` keeps each app's last 10 launch times in `Store`, each counting half as much per week since (`Apps.frecency`); in a search an app gets up to 250 points on top of its match, enough to order equally good matches by use but not to lift a scattered match above one at a word start. Power actions show under Power in All and System mode when the query matches their name or a common word for them (owner's request); all but Lock ask first: the results make way for a question with Cancel (chosen first) and the action, ←→ choose, Enter confirms, Esc or typing goes back. Ctrl+H J K L work like the arrow keys (vim motions, owner's request; plain letters still type). A query that is a calculation shows its answer at the top of All and Apps mode (`launcher/calc.js`, a small parser, no `eval`; tested by `tests/calc.test.js`); the answer is only shown, never copied (owner's call: no clipboard features). Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call launcher toggle`, `apps` / `windows` / `workspaces` / `system` / `bluetooth` (each opens its mode, or closes the launcher if it already shows it), `open <mode>`, `search <mode> <text>` |
+| Launcher | `Apps` (wraps `DesktopEntries`), `Compositor`, the services | `launcher/Launcher.qml` builds each mode as a flat list of rows: All (quick controls, open windows; with a query: apps, windows, system), Apps, Windows, Workspaces (cards with a mini map of the real layout), System (cards for audio, night light and Bluetooth, app volumes, output devices), and Bluetooth inside System (power switch, paired devices with connect/disconnect, nearby named devices with pair; Ctrl+Backspace forgets, not plain Backspace, which edits the search). It scans only while the Bluetooth view is open, for at most a minute. Pairing questions from the agent (`Bluetooth.request`, built after phase 8; not on a board) take the place of the results like a power question and open the launcher in the Bluetooth view: a code to compare, a code to type on the device (with a pistachio cursor under the next key), or "Pair with …?" / "Let … use audio?", with Cancel or Decline chosen first; a PIN or passkey is typed into the search field, which takes only valid characters then and is read-only during the other questions; Tab is ignored until it's answered, and Esc or closing the launcher declines. Fuzzy match with highlighted letters in `launcher/fuzzy.js` (tested by `tests/fuzzy.test.js`), which ignores case and accents (owner's request, after phase 8): `fold()` turns each character into its plain lower-case letter (NFD's first character, plus ł ø đ ħ ı ŧ, which don't decompose), one for one, so the indices still point into the text as written; plain ASCII skips that work. Windows and workspaces are snapshotted when it opens, so rows only change when you type or switch mode; the list is a plain array, because a `ScriptModel` left the previous mode's rows on screen. Terminal apps are wrapped in `$TERMINAL` (else kitty). System mode is built of cards (owner's request; the boards show plain rows): Audio (volume, brightness), Night light (a switch row with its status, warmth, a Schedule switch row and the two times), Do not disturb (the same, with a "Night light's times" switch that hides its own times; time rows carry their own get and set) and Bluetooth (a switch row with the connected device and battery, and "Scan for devices", which opens the Bluetooth view). A card's rows stay separate list rows, each drawing its slice of the card's outline, so ↑↓, ←→ (warmth ±250 K, times ±15 min; the wheel over a slider does the same) and Enter (switches) work as everywhere else; a query shows a whole card when it matches its name or any row. It also lists each app playing sound under Apps, with its own volume slider (←→, Enter mutes); the Audio card has a Microphone slider (Enter mutes); Input device lists the microphones, like Output device (Enter makes one the default, through `Pipewire.preferredDefaultAudioSource`); a System monitor card shows CPU (load, temperature, model), memory, an NVIDIA GPU (load, temperature, VRAM) and free disk space as meters in `sageBlue` that ease to each reading. Disks (owner's request, after phase 8; space only, no network speed) are `df` on / and /home, one row ("Disk") when they're the same device (here, btrfs subvolumes of one partition), else "System disk" and "Home"; the row turns terracotta under 10 % free. The rows come from `SystemStats.diskMounts`, which changes only when the mounts do, so a ticking figure doesn't rebuild the list. Its rows are readings, so the keys skip them, and `SystemStats` runs `services/stats.sh` every 1.5 s only while the card is on screen (owner's request, after phase 8). It ends with Shell settings, a card with the Drop shadows switch (owner's request; not on a board) and Quiet in fullscreen (owner's request, after phase 8: while the focused window is fullscreen, `Compositor.fullscreen` from its `Toplevel`, `Notifs.quiet` holds toasts back like Do not disturb, critical ones excepted; on by default, kept in `Store`). Apps mode opens with Frequent, the five apps launched most and most lately (owner's request, after phase 8): `Apps.launch` keeps each app's last 10 launch times in `Store`, each counting half as much per week since (`Apps.frecency`); in a search an app gets up to 250 points on top of its match, enough to order equally good matches by use but not to lift a scattered match above one at a word start. Power actions show under Power in All and System mode when the query matches their name or a common word for them (owner's request); all but Lock ask first: the results make way for a question with Cancel (chosen first) and the action, ←→ choose, Enter confirms, Esc or typing goes back. Ctrl+H J K L work like the arrow keys (vim motions, owner's request; plain letters still type). On a window row (owner's request, after phase 8), Ctrl+W or Ctrl+Q close it, Ctrl+F floats or tiles it, Ctrl+1–9 sends it to that workspace without following it; the launcher stays open, and the row shows the change once Hyprland reports it (a snapshot 300 ms later; "floating" in its subtitle). Files after a `/` (owner's request, after phase 8): `Files` runs `services/files.py` while that search shows, which lists the home folder once (`os.scandir`, ~25 ms for 22,000 entries; hidden entries, `node_modules`, `__pycache__` and the contents of any folder holding a `CACHEDIR.TAG`, such as Cargo's `target`, left out) and answers each query as a JSON line with the 60 best, ranked as `fuzzy.js` ranks (a regex of the letters in order finds the candidates); the launcher then runs `Fuzzy.match` on those for the highlights, keeping the last answer, narrowed to what still matches, until the next comes. Matching in QML was too slow: ~90 ms a keystroke for 22,000 names even as a bare loop (measured). A query with a `/` matches the path. Enter opens it with `xdg-open`, Shift+Enter its folder (FileManager1's ShowItems would pick the file, but it starts Nautilus here while folders open in hyprfm). Notifications after a `!` (owner's request, after phase 8): the summary matched first, with highlights, then the ones whose body or app contains the query (folded); a bare `!` lists them all under a Clear all row, which asks first like a power action (its question names it: `question` on the action). Enter shows one in the centre. A query that is a calculation shows its answer at the top of All and Apps mode (`launcher/calc.js`, a small parser, no `eval`; tested by `tests/calc.test.js`); the answer is only shown, never copied (owner's call: no clipboard features). Keybinds: `qs -c pistachio ipc call launcher toggle`, `apps` / `windows` / `workspaces` / `system` / `bluetooth` (each opens its mode, or closes the launcher if it already shows it), `open <mode>`, `search <mode> <text>` Emoji come up only after a `:` in All or Apps (owner's request, after phase 8): `launcher/emoji.js`, 1,906 fully-qualified emoji without skin tones (Unicode's emoji-test.txt 16.0, which Noto Color Emoji draws, with CLDR's English keywords; `extras/make-emoji.py` rebuilds it), as result rows with the emoji in the disc (`Theme.fontEmoji`), names matched before keywords; Enter copies it (`Clipboard`, wl-copy) and closes, and a bare `:` lists the 16 copied lately first, then all Commands after a `$` (owner's request, after phase 8): the typed one as a Run row and the recent ones that match (`Apps.recentCommands`, 20); Enter runs it in `kitty --hold`, Shift+Enter in the background through a detached `sh` that waits for it and then sends a notification, "Finished" or "Failed" with the exit code, and the last line it printed (owner's request) |
 
 ## Audio, Bluetooth and brightness
 
